{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EiegYN2RiMz46jp4xfVMZ2","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→\n     2→# Aquarium / Planted Tank Ecosystem Simulator — Implementation Spec (Rust)\n     3→\n     4→Version: 0.1  \n     5→Target system: freshwater planted shrimp tank (`Neocaridina davidi`)  \n     6→Primary UI: terminal / text UI  \n     7→Core requirement: scientifically grounded, deterministic, agent-implementable\n     8→\n     9→---\n    10→\n    11→## 1. Objective\n    12→\n    13→Build a Rust simulation engine that models a living freshwater planted aquarium as an ecosystem rather than a scripted set of events.\n    14→\n    15→The simulator must let a player:\n    16→\n    17→- configure tanks of different sizes and shapes\n    18→- stock and maintain a tank over time\n    19→- change husbandry variables, including **ambient temperature**\n    20→- observe chemistry, plant growth, algae, biofilm, microfauna, shrimp condition, and long-term stabilization\n    21→- encounter realistic emergent problems such as:\n    22→  - cycling delays\n    23→  - ammonia or nitrite stress\n    24→  - algae blooms\n    25→  - oxygen dips\n    26→  - breeding success/failure\n    27→  - molt issues\n    28→  - detritus accumulation\n    29→  - instability in small tanks compared with larger tanks\n    30→\n    31→The design must be specific enough that an implementation agent can build the system incrementally without re-architecting the codebase.\n    32→\n    33→---\n    34→\n    35→## 2. Scope\n    36→\n    37→## 2.1 In scope for v1\n    38→\n    39→A single freshwater tank containing:\n    40→\n    41→- water column\n    42→- tank geometry and water volume\n    43→- substrate\n    44→- filter / biomedia compartment\n    45→- heater (optional but supported)\n    46→- light\n    47→- rooted plants\n    48→- periphyton / biofilm\n    49→- free algae\n    50→- microbial guilds\n    51→- microfauna guilds\n    52→- shrimp population\n    53→- detritus / dissolved organics\n    54→- player maintenance actions\n    55→\n    56→## 2.2 Out of scope for v1\n    57→\n    58→- photorealistic graphics\n    59→- saltwater / reef chemistry\n    60→- disease-specific pathology models\n    61→- species-level microbial taxonomic simulation\n    62→- exact CFD, hydrodynamics, or molecular chemistry\n    63→- multiplayer\n    64→- online features\n    65→\n    66→## 2.3 Planned later\n    67→\n    68→- fish populations\n    69→- snails and other macro-grazers\n    70→- additional plant archetypes\n    71→- scenario editor\n    72→- richer rendering frontend\n    73→- more detailed carbonate chemistry and ion pairing\n    74→- external research calibration tools\n    75→\n    76→---\n    77→\n    78→## 3. Scientific modeling stance\n    79→\n    80→The simulation is **mechanistic and guild-based**.\n    81→\n    82→It must not attempt molecule-level or species-complete ecological simulation. Instead, it must model the main causal pathways that drive real aquarium outcomes:\n    83→\n    84→- waste generation -> ammonia -> nitrite -> nitrate\n    85→- pH and temperature effects on unionized ammonia risk\n    86→- oxygen and carbon cycling\n    87→- plant vs algae competition\n    88→- substrate and biofilm effects\n    89→- microfauna grazing and predation loops\n    90→- shrimp feeding, stress, molting, and reproduction\n    91→- tank-size-dependent stability\n    92→\n    93→Important scientific anchors for the model:\n    94→\n    95→- Unionized ammonia fraction increases with pH, and temperature affects ammonia toxicity.\n    96→- Home aquarium biofilters show real microbial succession and variable cycle times rather than one fixed “recipe.”\n    97→- Aquarium biofilters commonly include AOA and comammox `Nitrospira`, so nitrification should not be represented as instant or purely magical conversion.\n    98→- Submerged macrophytes can take nutrients from sediment and/or the water column.\n    99→- Rising temperature interacts with nutrient availability and can shift the plant/algae balance.\n   100→- Protozoan grazing affects bacterial communities and nutrient mineralization.\n   101→- `Neocaridina davidi` reproduction is temperature-sensitive; high temperatures can suppress ovarian maturation and spawning.\n   102→\n   103→---\n   104→\n   105→## 4. Design principles\n   106→\n   107→1. **Deterministic core**\n   108→   - same seed + same actions + same version = same result\n   109→\n   110→2. **Mass-balance first**\n   111→   - store masses/amounts internally where possible\n   112→   - derive concentrations for display and toxicity calculations\n   113→\n   114→3. **Tank size matters**\n   115→   - volume, footprint, depth, surface area, and wall area must affect the dynamics\n   116→\n   117→4. **Engine/UI separation**\n   118→   - core simulation crate must not depend on terminal or graphics code\n   119→\n   120→5. **Data-driven parameters**\n   121→   - species and process parameters loaded from data files\n   122→   - each important parameter carries provenance metadata\n   123→\n   124→6. **Progressive fidelity**\n   125→   - start with robust abstractions\n   126→   - refine later where science or gameplay demands it\n   127→\n   128→7. **Visible causality**\n   129→   - user should be able to inspect why the tank is improving or failing\n   130→\n   131→---\n   132→\n   133→## 5. Functional requirements\n   134→\n   135→The system MUST support:\n   136→\n   137→- different tank dimensions and fill heights\n   138→- different substrate types and depths\n   139→- at least one shrimp species pack\n   140→- at least two plant guilds\n   141→- at least two algae/biofilm guilds\n   142→- nutrient and oxygen cycling\n   143→- water temperature dynamics influenced by ambient temperature\n   144→- partial water changes with configurable source water\n   145→- feeding and detritus accumulation\n   146→- reproduction and juvenile survival for shrimp\n   147→- time acceleration (hours, days, weeks)\n   148→- logging of events and causes\n   149→- deterministic save/load\n   150→\n   151→The system SHOULD support:\n   152→\n   153→- seasonal ambient temperature schedules\n   154→- heater thermostat behavior\n   155→- filter cleaning side effects\n   156→- top-off water\n   157→- fertilizer dosing\n   158→- photoperiod editing\n   159→- different light strengths\n   160→- simple denitrification in oxygen-poor substrate microzones\n   161→\n   162→The system MAY support later:\n   163→\n   164→- fish\n   165→- snails\n   166→- disease\n   167→- graphical rendering\n   168→- multiplayer or shared scenarios\n   169→\n   170→---\n   171→\n   172→## 6. Tank size and geometry model\n   173→\n   174→Tank size is a first-class simulation input, not cosmetic metadata.\n   175→\n   176→## 6.1 Required geometry inputs\n   177→\n   178→```rust\n   179→pub struct TankGeometry {\n   180→    pub length_cm: f32,\n   181→    pub width_cm: f32,\n   182→    pub height_cm: f32,\n   183→    pub fill_height_cm: f32,\n   184→    pub glass_thickness_mm: f32,\n   185→    pub open_top: bool,\n   186→}\n   187→```\n   188→\n   189→## 6.2 Derived geometry quantities\n   190→\n   191→The engine MUST derive:\n   192→\n   193→- `water_volume_l`\n   194→- `surface_area_cm2` = length * width\n   195→- `footprint_area_cm2`\n   196→- `wall_area_cm2`\n   197→- `substrate_plan_area_cm2`\n   198→- `mean_depth_cm`\n   199→- `surface_area_to_volume_ratio`\n   200→- `wall_area_to_volume_ratio`\n   201→\n   202→## 6.3 Why geometry matters\n   203→\n   204→These derived values must affect simulation outcomes:\n   205→\n   206→- **Dilution / concentration**\n   207→  - same mass of feed in a 10 L tank causes a larger concentration spike than in 100 L\n   208→\n   209→- **Thermal inertia**\n   210→  - smaller tanks change temperature faster\n   211→\n   212→- **Heat exchange**\n   213→  - surface area and wall area affect passive exchange with ambient air\n   214→\n   215→- **Gas exchange**\n   216→  - open water surface area affects oxygenation / CO2 exchange\n   217→\n   218→- **Light distribution**\n   219→  - deeper tanks attenuate light more strongly with depth\n   220→\n   221→- **Plant carrying capacity**\n   222→  - footprint and substrate depth affect rooted plant biomass capacity\n   223→\n   224→- **Periphyton / biofilm area**\n   225→  - hardscape, glass area, and substrate expose colonizable surfaces\n   226→\n   227→- **Bioload stability**\n   228→  - small tanks are less forgiving; large tanks buffer fixed disturbances better\n   229→\n   230→## 6.4 Tank size classes\n   231→\n   232→The engine must not hardcode classes, but example presets should exist:\n   233→\n   234→- nano: 5–20 L\n   235→- small: 20–60 L\n   236→- medium: 60–200 L\n   237→- large: 200+ L\n   238→\n   239→The same scenario logic must run at any size.\n   240→\n   241→---\n   242→\n   243→## 7. Time model\n   244→\n   245→## 7.1 Base tick\n   246→\n   247→The engine MUST use a fixed-step simulation.\n   248→\n   249→Recommended v1 base tick:\n   250→- **1 hour per simulation tick**\n   251→\n   252→Optional implementation detail:\n   253→- allow internal substeps (`substeps_per_tick`) for future numerical stability without changing public behavior.\n   254→\n   255→## 7.2 Multi-scale update layers\n   256→\n   257→Use three logical clocks:\n   258→\n   259→### Hourly layer\n   260→- temperature\n   261→- gas exchange\n   262→- ammonia speciation\n   263→- oxygen changes\n   264→- decomposition\n   265→- nitrification\n   266→- feeding pulses\n   267→- excretion\n   268→- light exposure accumulation\n   269→\n   270→### Daily layer\n   271→- plant growth\n   272→- algae growth\n   273→- biofilm maturation\n   274→- shrimp condition/stress\n   275→- molting progress\n   276→- egg development\n   277→- juvenile recruitment\n   278→- mortality checks\n   279→\n   280→### Weekly/monthly layer\n   281→- ecological succession indicators\n   282→- substrate enrichment/depletion\n   283→- biofilter maturity trends\n   284→- long-term stability metrics\n   285→- seasonal ambient patterns\n   286→\n   287→## 7.3 Determinism\n   288→\n   289→All stochastic events must use an explicit RNG object seeded from scenario state.\n   290→\n   291→```rust\n   292→pub struct SimSeed(pub u64);\n   293→```\n   294→\n   295→No direct calls to thread-local RNG are permitted inside the core engine.\n   296→\n   297→---\n   298→\n   299→## 8. Simulation state model\n   300→\n   301→## 8.1 Core state container\n   302→\n   303→```rust\n   304→pub struct TankState {\n   305→    pub meta: SimMeta,\n   306→    pub geometry: TankGeometry,\n   307→    pub environment: EnvironmentState,\n   308→    pub hardware: HardwareState,\n   309→    pub water: WaterState,\n   310→    pub substrate: Vec<SubstrateLayerState>,\n   311→    pub filter: FilterState,\n   312→    pub plants: Vec<PlantGuildState>,\n   313→    pub algae: AlgaeState,\n   314→    pub microbes: MicrobeState,\n   315→    pub microfauna: MicrofaunaState,\n   316→    pub animals: AnimalState,\n   317→    pub detritus: DetritusState,\n   318→    pub schedules: ScheduleState,\n   319→    pub observables: ObservableState,\n   320→    pub events: Vec<SimEvent>,\n   321→    pub rng: SimRng,\n   322→}\n   323→```\n   324→\n   325→## 8.2 Environment state\n   326→\n   327→```rust\n   328→pub struct EnvironmentState {\n   329→    pub ambient_temp_c: f32,\n   330→    pub ambient_temp_schedule: AmbientSchedule,\n   331→    pub room_light_leak: f32, // 0..1, optional future use\n   332→    pub season_day: u16,\n   333→}\n   334→```\n   335→\n   336→### Ambient temperature action\n   337→Ambient temperature must be editable by the player at runtime. This represents room heating/cooling, seasonal weather, AC failure, or relocation of the tank.\n   338→\n   339→This action must affect:\n   340→- water temperature trends\n   341→- heater load\n   342→- dissolved oxygen saturation\n   343→- plant/algae rates\n   344→- shrimp stress and reproduction\n   345→- ammonia risk indirectly through temperature and pH-sensitive chemistry\n   346→\n   347→## 8.3 Hardware state\n   348→\n   349→```rust\n   350→pub struct HardwareState {\n   351→    pub light: LightState,\n   352→    pub heater: Option<HeaterState>,\n   353→    pub filter_pump: FilterPumpState,\n   354→    pub aeration: AerationState,\n   355→}\n   356→```\n   357→\n   358→### Light\n   359→```rust\n   360→pub struct LightState {\n   361→    pub enabled: bool,\n   362→    pub photoperiod_hours: f32,\n   363→    pub intensity_index: f32,      // relative scalar for v1\n   364→    pub par_surface_umol: f32,     // optional if known\n   365→    pub schedule_start_hour: u8,\n   366→}\n   367→```\n   368→\n   369→### Heater\n   370→```rust\n   371→pub struct HeaterState {\n   372→    pub enabled: bool,\n   373→    pub setpoint_c: f32,\n   374→    pub max_watts: f32,\n   375→    pub deadband_c: f32,\n   376→    pub efficiency: f32,\n   377→}\n   378→```\n   379→\n   380→### Filter / aeration\n   381→```rust\n   382→pub struct FilterPumpState {\n   383→    pub flow_lph: f32,\n   384→    pub media_surface_area_m2: f32,\n   385→    pub cleanliness: f32,  // 0..1\n   386→}\n   387→\n   388→pub struct AerationState {\n   389→    pub enabled: bool,\n   390→    pub intensity: f32,    // relative scalar\n   391→}\n   392→```\n   393→\n   394→## 8.4 Water state\n   395→\n   396→Internal water chemistry should primarily store **amounts** plus some derived state. Concentrations are derived from amount / volume.\n   397→\n   398→```rust\n   399→pub struct WaterState {\n   400→    pub volume_l: f32,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01YNVA7FgmkLcCKLLvttQAbz","session_id":"7c0cd9fe-375d-4881-87c2-86b174651532","uuid":"02be9bb3-07a5-474a-999b-b7e1e5197212"}
